---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import PanelHead from '../../components/PanelHead.astro';

// Self-hosted in /public/photos — my own photos, pulled from Unsplash (halfcadence)
// after source.unsplash.com was retired. Shot with iPhones, a Fuji X100, and a Leica Q.
//
// `w`/`h` are the file's REAL pixel dimensions, measured from the JPEG headers, not a
// shared guess — the browser needs the true ratio to reserve the right box, and three of
// these are not 1400×933 (06 is 927 tall, 10 is 934). One wrong height is a visible reflow
// on a slow connection.
//
// Alt text describes what is actually in the frame. Several of these filenames are
// inherited from the Unsplash export and DON'T match their image (01 isn't a volcano;
// 08 is a scooter, not a motorcycle) — the alt follows the photograph, not the filename.
const photos = [
  { src: '/photos/01-irazu-volcano.jpg', w: 1400, h: 933, alt: 'White concrete architecture and a flight of stairs under a blue sky' },
  { src: '/photos/02-green-mailbox-pink-wall.jpg', w: 1400, h: 933, alt: 'Green mailbox beside a pink wall' },
  { src: '/photos/04-chairs-on-grass.jpg', w: 1400, h: 933, alt: 'Grey chairs on a grass field' },
  { src: '/photos/05-woman-on-stairs.jpg', w: 1400, h: 933, alt: 'A figure leaning against a wall beneath a stone bridge by a river' },
  { src: '/photos/07-butterfly-brick-wall.jpg', w: 1400, h: 933, alt: 'A butterfly perched on a brick wall' },
  { src: '/photos/08-puppy-on-motorcycle.jpg', w: 1400, h: 933, alt: 'A grey toy poodle sitting on the footboard of a scooter' },
  { src: '/photos/09-woman-red-top.jpg', w: 1400, h: 933, alt: 'A passer-by in a red top' },
  { src: '/photos/03.jpg', w: 1400, h: 933, alt: 'Trees, benches, and a flowerbed along a park path' },
  { src: '/photos/10.jpg', w: 1400, h: 934, alt: 'A figure photographing from a fort wall under a wide cloudy sky' },
  { src: '/photos/06.jpg', w: 1400, h: 927, alt: 'A cello lying on a floor, in black and white' },
  { src: '/photos/11.jpg', w: 1400, h: 933, alt: 'A tan short-haired dog standing on an ornate marble floor' },
  { src: '/photos/12.jpg', w: 1400, h: 933, alt: 'A string quartet posed against a stone museum wall' },
];

// Two ragged column-flows, not a 2-up grid: odds left, evens right. Splitting the list
// here (rather than letting a grid pair them into rows) is what lets the two columns run
// independently, so no two photos are forced to share a baseline.
const left = photos.filter((_, i) => i % 2 === 0);
const right = photos.filter((_, i) => i % 2 === 1);
---

<Layout
  title="Photography — Andrew Shiau"
  description="Photographs shot with iPhones, a Fuji X100, and a Leica Q."
>
  <!-- THE SPLIT (chooser round 2: concept 02, scope 03). Same `.pin` /
       `.splitrule` / `.feed` children at the same 4:8 columns as the index, so the
       site's one vertical division doesn't move when you click into a case study. -->
  <div class="grid">
    <!-- NO LEDGER (annotation: "i wonder if this shuld not have top matter and just have the
         'shot with' line"). The panel is the marker, the link home, and the title — nothing else.
         WHY THE LEDGER WENT RATHER THAN THE DEK, because a second annotation asked for the
         opposite ("this is useless since there's a camera section delete"): the two rows and the
         dek were the SAME FACT twice, so one had to go, and the sentence is the better carrier.
         `Cameras: iPhones, Fuji X100, Leica Q.` and `Shot with a Leica, a Fuji, and an iPhone.`
         are one sentence and one table row saying one thing; the sentence reads at a glance and
         needs no label to explain what it is. `Contents: 12 photographs` counted what is directly
         below it — the page IS the count. And `Type: Photographs` was the row that prompted "seems
         sus": the title again, with a suffix.
         What it costs, stated: this is the one Type C page with no ledger, so the type now has two
         shapes (`recipes` keeps its counts). That is a real widening of the page-type system and it
         is recorded in STYLE.md, not just here — a type that quietly grows a second shape is how
         the eight pages drifted in the first place. -->
    <PanelHead href="/work/photography/" title="Photography" ledger={false} />

    <div class="feed">
      <!-- c1–12 of the FEED's twelve sub-tracks, not c1–8: the dek's own 32em cap is
           narrower than 8 of the feed's 700px, so c1–8 would set a column the text
           never reaches. No top margin — the dek is the top of the reading column. -->
      <div class="grid">
        <!-- Annotation: "can it just be 'shot with Leica, Fujifilm, Nikon, iPhone'". Taken, with
             one correction to keep it true — there is no Nikon in the set; the ledger reads
             iPhones, Fuji X100, Leica Q, and the dek must not claim a camera that didn't take
             any of these twelve. Naming a camera I didn't use would be the one fabricated fact on
             a page whose whole content is evidence.
             What it replaced: "Twelve photographs, shot over about a decade on whatever I had
             with me." Both halves were already on screen — `Contents: 12 photographs` and
             `Cameras: iPhones, Fuji X100, Leica Q` sit in the ledger directly above it — so the
             dek was the count and the kit a second time, in vaguer words. -->
        <p class="dek c1-12">Shot with a Leica, a Fuji, and an iPhone.</p>
      </div>


      <div class="grid" style="margin-top:calc(var(--unit)*1.5)">
        <div class="g-rag">
          <div class="col-l">
            {left.map((p, i) => (
              <figure>
                <img src={p.src} alt={p.alt} width={p.w} height={p.h}
                     loading={i === 0 ? 'eager' : 'lazy'} />
              </figure>
            ))}
          </div>
          <div class="col-r">
            {right.map((p) => (
              <figure>
                <img src={p.src} alt={p.alt} width={p.w} height={p.h} loading="lazy" />
              </figure>
            ))}
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
